One more detail: `--headless=new` — is it supported in Chrome 13x? In newer Chrome versions `--headless=new` was renamed; plain `--headless` now means the new headless (since Chrome 112 or later, `--headless=new` is deprecated → `--headless` = new mode). In the latest Chrome (2026), plain `--headless` is the new headless. Use `--headless=new`? If it's unrecognized it errors... Chrome usually ignores unrecognized flags. Safer: plain `--headless` (the new headless since 132; older versions treat it as old headless, but CDP still works; rAF in old headless: in old headless rAF might not fire without virtual time — but the RO callback doesn't require rAF. updateBounds is called directly from the RO callback. Old headless also fires RO callbacks... I think. To be safe, my resize assertion doesn't depend on rAF. And in the latest Chrome, plain --headless = new mode with full rAF. No problem.)

Also add `--no-first-run --no-default-browser-check --disable-gpu` (no GPU needed; --disable-gpu avoids driver issues in headless) and `--user-data-dir=<tmpdir>` (required to use remote debugging with a custom profile to avoid conflicts with a running Chrome instance).

rAF in new headless: does it fire without `--run-all-compositor-stages-before-draw`? In new headless, rAF works normally (it renders). sleep(400) covers it either way.

